{
  "question": "For \\(a>1\\), evaluate \\(\\displaystyle I(a)=\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}\\frac{dx}{(a+\\cos x)^2}\\).",
  "options": {
    "A": "\\(\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi a}{(a^2-1)^{3/2}}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi}{(a^2-1)^{3/2}}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi a}{(a^2-1)^{3/2}}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi a}{\\sqrt{a^2-1}}\\)"
  },
  "answer": "A",
  "explanation": "Define \\(J(a)=\\int_{0}^{2\\pi}\\frac{dx}{a+\\cos x}\\). Using the Weierstrass substitution \\(t=\\tan(x/2)\\), with \\(\\cos x=(1-t^2)/(1+t^2)\\) and \\(dx=2dt/(1+t^2)\\), one obtains \\(J(a)=\\frac{2\\pi}{\\sqrt{a^2-1}}\\). Differentiation under the integral sign gives \\(J'(a)=-I(a)\\). Therefore, \\(I(a)=-J'(a)=\\frac{2\\pi a}{(a^2-1)^{3/2}}\\)."
}

