  "question": "In the Ramadge–Wonham supervisory-control framework, consider a fully observed plant with prefix-closed language L(G) = {ε, a, b, au, bu}. The controllable and uncontrollable event sets are Σ_c = {a, b} and Σ_uc = {u}, respectively. Thus, after either a or b, the plant may execute u, which a supervisor cannot disable. A safety specification is given by the prefix-closed language K = {ε, a, b, bu} ⊆ L(G). Considering only safety behavior, which statement correctly characterizes the supremal controllable sublanguage supC(K) and the initial action of the maximally permissive supervisor?",
  "options": {
    "A": "supC(K) = K, so both a and b may remain enabled initially; the supervisor need only disable u after a occurs.",
    "B": "K is not controllable because a ∈ K, au ∈ L(G), and au ∉ K, although u is uncontrollable. Therefore, supC(K) = {ε, b, bu}, and the supervisor must disable a initially while leaving b enabled.",
    "C": "K is not controllable, but supC(K) = {ε, a, b}; the supervisor must leave both a and b enabled initially and disable u after either event.",
