    "D": "Collusion cannot be sustained for any δ < 1 because the one-period deviation profit exceeds the collusive profit."
  },
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Explanation": "Each firm's per-period collusive profit is πC = a^2/8. In the Cournot punishment equilibrium, each firm produces a/3 and earns πN = a^2/9. If a firm deviates while its rival produces a/4, its optimal output is 3a/8, yielding deviation profit πD = 9a^2/64. Sustaining collusion requires πC/(1 − δ) ≥ πD + δπN/(1 − δ), which simplifies to δ ≥ (πD − πC)/(πD − πN) = 9/17. Because all three profits are proportional to a^2, changes in a do not affect this threshold. Permanent Cournot reversion is credible because it specifies a Nash equilibrium in every punishment period."
}

