        "Provide a partially completed residual-network worksheet and a glossary of flow terminology.",
        "Use small integral capacities and require students to label forward residual capacity and reverse residual capacity separately.",
        "Supply an algorithm-selection checklist covering directedness, cycles, weights, and required output."
      ],
      "extension": [
        "Ask students to prove the termination and integrality arguments for integral capacities.",
        "Introduce Dinic's algorithm and its improved behavior on important special networks.",
        "Challenge students to identify invalid flow reductions, including reductions that lose integrality or fail to preserve constraints."
      ]
    },
    "teaching_resources": [
      "Slides containing graph representations, algorithm preconditions, and complexity comparisons",
      "Printed graph cards and residual-network worksheets",
      "Two writing colors or distinct line styles for original and residual edges, with text labels so interpretation does not depend on color alone",
      "A programming environment and automated lab tests",
      "Optional graph or flow visualization software, used alongside hand tracing rather than as a replacement for it"
    ],
    "instructor_notes": [
      "Emphasize that Ford-Fulkerson is a general method, while Edmonds-Karp specifies BFS augmentation.",
      "Do not present a simulation alone as a correctness proof; connect implementation invariants to capacity, conservation, and residual reachability.",
      "The maximum-flow value is unique, but maximum-flow assignments and minimum cuts need not be unique.",
      "When calculating cut capacity, include only original edges directed from S to T, not residual edges or edges directed from T to S.",
      "Continuously connect new problems to modeling: the central skill is not merely running an algorithm, but constructing a model whose solution can be translated back correctly."
    ]
  }
